                <p>Ad budget allocation is one of the core decisions in landing page operations. Get the budget wrong, and even the best creatives won't save your campaigns. This checklist breaks down allocation logic into actionable steps so you know exactly where your money goes—and when to scale or cut.</p>
<h2>1. Two Phases, Two Strategies</h2>
<p>Budget allocation isn't one-size-fits-all. Different stages require different approaches.</p>
<h3>Testing Phase (0-3 months): Validate First</h3>
<p>The goal here is finding product-creative combos that convert. Keep budgets small—even if you have a $100K monthly budget, reserve only 20-30% for testing.</p>
<ul>
<li>Cap each product/ad set at $50/day initially</li>
<li>Test no more than 5 ad sets simultaneously</li>
<li>Run each channel test for at least 7 days before judging results</li>
<li>Prioritize validating the conversion funnel before scaling</li>
</ul>
<h3>Scaling Phase (3+ months): Replicate Winners, Maximize ROI</h3>
<p>Once you've found a profitable model, scale it. Shift budget toward validated channels and creatives.</p>
<ul>
<li>Allocate 70%+ of budget to ad sets hitting ROAS ≥ 3x</li>
<li>Reserve 20% for testing new creatives or audiences</li>
<li>Set auto-rules: automatically lower bids or pause any ad set with ROAS below 2x for 3 consecutive days</li>
</ul>
<h2>2. Google vs. Meta Budget Split Reference</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Channel</th>
<th>Best For</th>
<th>Recommended %</th>
<th>Starting Budget</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Meta (Facebook + Instagram)</td>
<td>Visual, impulse purchases, fashion/accessories/home</td>
<td>50-70%</td>
<td>$20/day/ad set</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Google Search</td>
<td>High intent categories, tools/electronics/beauty</td>
<td>20-30%</td>
<td>$15/day/ad set</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>YouTube/Display</td>
<td>Brand awareness, retargeting</td>
<td>10-20%</td>
<td>$10/day/ad set</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>TikTok</td>
<td>Younger audience, trend-driven products, low-friction categories</td>
<td>10-30%</td>
<td>$15/day/ad set</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Tip</strong>: These ratios aren't fixed. Adjust based on where your target audience actually spends time and your own performance data.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>3. 10 Execution Checkpoints</h2>
<p>Run through this list every week during your review:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Are you sticking to your planned channel split? Is any channel off by more than 20%?</li>
<li>[ ] Are testing-phase ad sets still running? Should you pause underperformers and reallocate budget?</li>
<li>[ ] Do ROAS ≥ 3x ad sets account for 70%+ of your scaling-phase spend?</li>
<li>[ ] Have you reserved 10-20% of budget as a "safety margin" for new creative tests?</li>
<li>[ ] Have you set daily caps on each platform to prevent runaway spend?</li>
<li>[ ] Are any channels underfunded, causing frequent learning phase resets?</li>
<li>[ ] Does your retargeting cover add-to-cart but no-purchase users?</li>
<li>[ ] Did you increase key channel budgets 3-5 days before holidays or sales events?</li>
<li>[ ] Are all platform account balances sufficient to avoid delivery interruptions?</li>
<li>[ ] Have you logged this week's CAC by channel and overall ROAS for next week's decisions?</li>
</ul>
<h2>4. Quick FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: Should I split budget by channel or by product?</strong></p>
<p>Channel-first is clearer when starting out—it makes cross-platform comparison easier. Shift to product-based allocation only when product lines differ significantly (different price points or minimal audience overlap).</p>
<p><strong>Q: One channel has great ROAS. Can I move all other budget there?</strong></p>
<p>Not recommended. Over-concentration on a single channel leads to audience exhaustion and rising CPCs. Multi-channel distribution spreads risk and stabilizes acquisition.</p>
<p><strong>Q: How long until budget changes show results?</strong></p>
<p>Platform algorithms need 3-5 days to relearn. Give any adjustment at least 7 days before evaluating—don't panic over 2-3 day fluctuations.</p>
<hr />
<p>There's no perfect formula for budget allocation, but there is a reliable framework: validate first, scale what works, adjust dynamically. Keep this checklist handy for your weekly reviews and you'll avoid the trial-and-error that burns through budgets.</p>
